The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if (!(BASE_TABLE(dfa)[i] & MATCH_FLAG_DIFF_ENCODE) &&
    (DEFAULT_TABLE(dfa)[i] >= state_count))
    goto out;
```
Reason: The condition only verifies the bounds of `DEFAULT_TABLE[i]` when the state is **not** differentially encoded. During the subsequent differential‑encoding loop, `DEFAULT_TABLE[j]` is read and used as an index (e.g., `k = DEFAULT_TABLE(dfa)[j]` followed by `BASE_TABLE(dfa)[j] |= MARK_DIFF_ENCODE` acting as a write via array indexing) without any bounds check. A maliciously crafted DFA can set `DEFAULT_TABLE[j] >= state_count`, causing out‑of‑bounds reads and writes.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (DEFAULT_TABLE(dfa)[i] >= state_count) {
    pr_err("AppArmor DFA default state out of bounds");
    goto out;
}
```
Reason: The condition `!(BASE_TABLE(dfa)[i] & MATCH_FLAG_DIFF_ENCODE)` is removed so that **all** `DEFAULT_TABLE[i]` entries are now validated against `state_count`, regardless of the differential‑encoding flag. This ensures that when the later loop reads `DEFAULT_TABLE[j]` it will always be a valid index, preventing the out‑of‑bounds access. An error message is also added for clarity.

